Subject: =?UTF-8?q?=F0=9F=94=97=20Ask-only=20links:=20a=20linked=20machine?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20may=20ask=20opted-in=20threads=20and=20read=20the=20answers?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Spec: fleet docs/t3-ask-link-spec.md. Upstream proposal:
pingdotgg/t3code discussion #40978.

Behaviours:
- New link access level "ask", below read-only: CLI
  `t3 environment link --access ask`, the link dialog's picker ("Ask only"),
  the link-request card on web and mobile, and every schema that lists link
  access levels. An ask session holds only the new scope orchestration:ask,
  so nothing guarded by orchestration:read opens to it; approving one, or
  making its link code, needs orchestration:read. Approval codes get the
  ASK- prefix. The level is for linked environments only: outside-app
  pickers hide it and approval refuses it for other clients. A machine
  advertises the capability askLinks; linking at ask to a machine without it
  is refused before any request.
- An ask link is made only with a link code
  (`t3 auth pairing create --link ask`). A general pairing code presented
  for an ask link is refused and spent; the link dialog and the
  link-request cards show the command to run.
- Per-thread opt-in "Answer linked questions" in the thread menu (web header
  and sidebar menus, mobile thread row), off by default, off on forks, side
  chats and subagent threads. It is the command thread.linked-questions.set,
  which needs orchestration:operate and access:write and has no MCP tool,
  palette entry or keybinding. While it is on, the chat header shows
  "Answers linked questions".
- An ask session may call three tools and nothing else: t3_ask_list (threads
  that opted in: id, title, machine label), t3_ask_send (one plain-text
  question, at most 8 000 characters) and t3_ask_wait (the answer text of
  that question, at most 20 000 characters, only for the asking link's own
  question). Every other tool refuses it in the shared access declarations
  (McpToolAccess), tool and event discovery hides everything else (MCP
  milestone events included), the link thread routes answer 403 ask_link,
  and the asking machine refuses other tools on an ask link before anything
  leaves it. A link of any level may use the three tools. Errors use a fixed
  vocabulary; a thread that does not answer and a missing thread get the
  same reply.
- Each question is answered in its own thread. t3_ask_send creates a thread
  in the asked thread's project, marked "From <machine>" (linkOrigin) and
  tied to the asked thread by the server-only field linkedQuestionOf, titled
  with the question, with the question as its one message and one run. The
  asked thread itself gets no message, run, queue entry, event or state
  change from a question. A replayed clientRequestId returns the earlier
  question when it names the same asked thread.
- What the answer sees: the asked thread's conversation, read when the
  answering turn starts and never stored: the messages its owner typed and
  the assistant's text, newest kept up to 32 000 characters. Messages from
  agents, other threads, links, scheduled tasks, workflows, guests and
  compaction notes are left out. Scheduled-task webhook URL tokens are
  withheld from that context, from answers and from the titles t3_ask_list
  returns. The question is inert text: no slash or local command handling,
  no mention or context expansion, no attachments.
- The answering turn runs without tools, enforced when the provider turn
  starts; only a question thread can run one. Claude answers with an empty
  tool list, permission mode dontAsk and a denying permission callback, no
  MCP servers (strict config), verbatim prompts, slash commands off, hooks
  off, no project or local settings, no launch-argument settings or prompt
  files, no git context, no additional directories or workspace folder list,
  no stored session, in an empty T3-owned folder, and needs Claude Code
  2.1.248 or later; tool calls the model writes are neither run nor shown.
  User-level Claude settings still apply (they carry the machine's endpoint
  and credential), so the user-level CLAUDE.md and rules reach an answer. A
  provider without tools.supportsToollessTurns (every other one today)
  refuses the question before a session opens.
- A question thread takes nothing after its question: later messages, the
  opt-in, forks and side chats, pull-request and issue links and watches,
  adopting a folder, child threads and delegation are refused; it is never
  continued after a restart or a usage limit, takes no snapshots and runs no
  project scripts. Clients block its composer, hide the opt-in and "Turn
  into project" there and never offer its folder as a worktree.
- Limits, all answered with the retryable code rate_limited: 10 questions
  per 10 minutes per link (in memory), one open question per asked thread,
  20 unarchived question threads per asked thread.
- A question the provider stops on a usage limit fails with "The thread hit
  its usage limit before answering. Ask again later."
- New server setting linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess (default
  full-access), set in Settings → Environments → List ("Links into this
  machine") and lockable through the managed policy; changing it needs
  access:write. A link above it is refused at approval (before the pairing
  code is spent) and at token exchange, and an existing link above it is
  refused and revoked on its next use at every entry a link token has (/mcp,
  the link thread routes including open streams, client pairing). A link
  that was above the cap stays refused after the cap is raised and after a
  restart: the server keeps, in its secret store, when each level was last
  above the cap. A history that cannot be read counts every level above ask
  as above the cap at that start. Linking a machine again ends the previous
  link's session at the addresses that link used.
- The environments map and its access meter draw six levels; the five old
  levels look as before.
- User docs (docs/user/remote-access.md) and internal docs describe the
  level, the opt-in, question threads, what an answer sees, the cap and
  tools.supportsToollessTurns. New strings are English; no translation
  catalog is changed.

Not covered: the mobile settings screen has no row for the cap; t3_ask_list
and t3_ask_wait have no rate limit of their own; the evals cases added under
evals/mcp-tool-use were not run against a model; question text is the
thread's title and shows in notifications.

Port to a6c69be8df: McpOAuth.ts: upstream cc2e4c1704 (#41884, revoking all other sessions also ends sign-ins still in progress) reads the revocation count at the start of approve and mints the code with it; that read now comes before this commit's ask-only and inbound-cap checks, so an ask link's OAuth sign-in approved before "revoke all others" gets no session, like any other sign-in; both inbound-cap checks stay. McpOAuth.test.ts: upstream's case runs for an app and an ask link. ClaudeAdapterV2.ts: upstream ff6b10e0a0 (#41928) reports the mcp startup step inside this commit's mcpServers branch after the shared boot wait, so a tool-less question turn, which has no MCP servers, reports none and keeps its verbatim-prompts version check; its options (no tools, no MCP servers, no hooks) are unchanged next to upstream 74382f2530 (#39729) and e632155b3a (#41816). agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is 62907 as AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures it on upstream's smaller tool list (69d8f4217f, #41809).

Port to 54ba2408f4: ContextHandoffService.ts: upstream 854f02bc2d (#42114) stops fork handoffs at the latest /clear (a started, non-scheduled freshContext run) and then drops ancestor history; readOwnHistoryItems keeps this commit's turnItemTypes filter under upstream's boundary, so readLinkedQuestionContext, which reads the asked thread through readForkHistoryItems, now gives a question only the asked thread's conversation since its latest /clear, without the history it inherited from a fork before it. ContextHandoffService.test.ts: upstream's /clear cases sit next to this commit's cases, plus a case for a linked question on a cleared, forked thread. McpToolAccess.test.ts: upstream 2fe7febc8d (#41977) adds writesOwnerWork, which workflow runs now use; it is not an asks declaration, so the default deny refuses it to an ask link (a new row checks it), and upstream's probe tool and rows sit next to this commit's. docs/internals/04_environment-auth.md: the ask-grant bullet follows upstream's workflow-run sentence (#41977, kept by 110ed776c9 #42016) and upstream's project-scripts bullet (bba3918e3c, #41979). agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is the value AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures on the carried tree.

Port to b8be2287dc: ContextHandoffService.ts: upstream a22a4f9bba (#30044, preserve inherited fork history in handoffs after rewind) reads a fork ancestor's runs by the fork's recorded membership (getForkSourceRunIds, migration 116_ForkHistoryMembership), so runs the source rewound after the fork still count; readOwnHistoryItems now takes the reading fork. readLinkedQuestionContext reads the asked thread the way upstream's own-thread handoff does: its own runs with no fork filter (fork null, rewound runs stay hidden) since its latest /clear, then, only when no /clear cut them off, readInheritedHandoffItems, which applies the membership rule to each ancestor; readOwnHistoryItems and readInheritedHandoffItems keep this commit's turnItemTypes filter, and readForkHistoryItems is upstream's again. ContextHandoffService.test.ts: the linked-question stubs provide getForkSourceRunIds, and a new case asks a fork whose source was rewound after the fork and sees the inherited rows, not the run rewound before the fork, the source's newer run or the fork's own rewound run. thread-list-v2-items.tsx keeps upstream 78de041d48's (#24917) offline triage (triageOnly) next to this commit's canSetLinkedQuestions and imports useAtomValue once. settings.ts keeps upstream c6162da7ec's (#42353) workflowSkippedRunsKept next to linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess. docs/internals/04_environment-auth.md: the ask-grant bullet follows upstream 924d9c1fff's (#42386) worktree layout command bullet. agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is 63112 as AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures it on the carried tree. client-runtime commandPermissions.test.ts passes upstream's new third argument of createThreadEnvironmentAtoms (the shell status atom, live here), without which the linked-questions command failed with a TypeError.

Port to 221953fbf3: useThreadActionMenu.ts: upstream 95411e3cd7 (#40742, thread actions for the current thread from cmd+k) builds the menu in readMenu; the linked-questions support, linkedQuestionsEnabled and isLinkedQuestion now sit in readMenu's supports and buildThreadActionMenuItems call, and the linked-questions case runs in its run(action) with readMenu's thread, so the item reaches upstream's cmd+k rows for the current thread through the same decision and permission check. agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is 63253 as AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures it on the carried tree, after upstream 3b5033a28c (#42506) added the workflow question withdraw tool text.

