+### Let linked machines ask a thread
+
+A thread answers questions from linked machines only after you turn on **Answer
+linked questions** in its thread menu. It is off by default. Only a session that
+manages access can change it; agents, links and scheduled tasks cannot. While it
+is on, the thread's header shows **Answers linked questions**.
+
+- Agents on a linked machine list these threads with `t3_ask_list`, ask one with
+  `t3_ask_send` and read the reply with `t3_ask_wait`. A link at any level can
+  ask; an **Ask only** link can do nothing else.
+- Each question is answered in its own new thread in the same project, marked
+  **From** and the asking machine's name. The question is its title, so it also
+  shows in notifications. Nothing is added to the thread you opted in, and its
+  queue, snooze and settled state stay as they were.
+- The answer sees the newest of that thread's messages that fit a limit, the
+  ones you typed and its replies, and nothing else from it: no files, command
+  output, attachments, guest messages, automation prompts, or messages from
+  agents and other threads. It answers in
+  text with every tool off: no shell, files, MCP servers, browser, web or
+  subagents. It may decline.
+- Claude answers with your user-level Claude settings, so your global
+  `CLAUDE.md` reaches it too; project files, project settings, git state and
+  hooks do not.
+- A question's thread takes no messages after its question, and cannot be
+  forked or asked itself. It turns unread when the answer is done and stays
+  until auto-settle or you settle, archive or delete it. You can also stop it.
+- Only threads running on Claude can answer; on other providers the question is
+  refused.
+- Each link may ask 10 questions in 10 minutes. A thread takes one question at a
+  time, and refuses more while 20 of its question threads are not archived.
+- A question stopped by a usage limit or a restart fails and is not resumed. Ask
+  again later.
+- Turning the setting off refuses questions that have not started yet.
+
+### Limit links into a machine
+
+**Links into this machine** in **Settings → Environments → Linked environments**
+(the **List** view) sets the broadest access another machine may link here with.
+It defaults to Full access. A pairing above it fails and names the limit. A link
+already above it stops working and stays stopped even if you raise the limit
+later; link it again within the limit. A [managed policy](./managed-policy.md#settings)
+can set and lock it with the key `linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess`, for
+example `{ "value": "ask", "locked": true }`.
 
